    "id": "geo-grain-size-distribution",
    "title": "Grain-Size Distribution Curve",
    "field": "Geology / Seismology",
    "plotType": "Semi-log cumulative curve (% finer vs. log grain diameter)",
    "description": "A grain-size distribution curve plots the cumulative percentage of a sediment sample finer than a given diameter against grain diameter on a logarithmic axis, conventionally drawn with coarse grains on the left. It is the standard output of a sieve-and-hydrometer (mechanical) analysis. A steep, near-vertical curve means the grains are nearly all one size (well sorted / poorly graded), while a gentle S-shaped sweep means a wide mix of sizes (well graded). Engineers and sedimentologists read particle statistics straight off the curve.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Grain diameter",
        "unit": "mm"
      },
      {
        "name": "Percent finer (passing)",
        "unit": "%"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "Reading the diameters at 10%, 30%, and 60% finer (D10, D30, D60) yields the uniformity coefficient Cu = D60/D10 and curvature coefficient Cc, which classify the soil as well graded or poorly graded. A steep curve flags a uniform, potentially unstable sand; a broad, smooth curve indicates a dense, well-graded mix good for compaction, and the fine-grained tail warns of clay that governs permeability and frost behaviour.",
