    "id": "geo-mohrs-circle",
    "title": "Mohr's Circle",
    "field": "Geology / Seismology",
    "plotType": "Circle in normal-stress vs. shear-stress space",
    "description": "Mohr's circle is a graphical representation of the state of stress at a point, drawn in a plane of normal stress (x-axis) versus shear stress (y-axis). Every possible plane through the point maps to a point on a circle whose diameter spans the maximum and minimum principal stresses. In structural geology and rock mechanics it is combined with a failure envelope (the Mohr–Coulomb line) to predict whether and on which plane a rock will fracture.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Maximum principal stress (σ1)",
        "unit": "MPa"
      },
      {
        "name": "Minimum principal stress (σ3)",
        "unit": "MPa"
      },
      {
        "name": "Rock cohesion",
        "unit": "MPa"
      },
      {
        "name": "Internal friction angle",
        "unit": "degrees"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "The circle's center gives the mean of the principal stresses, and its radius gives the maximum shear stress — half the differential stress (σ1−σ3), which equals the full diameter. If the circle just touches the failure envelope, the rock is at the point of failure, and the tangent point's angle identifies the orientation of the fault or fracture plane that will form. A circle shifted right (higher confining pressure) is more stable; an enlarging circle approaches failure.",
